**Definition 9.3 (remaining relational targets and boundaries).**
The broad target is a parsimonious generation structure expressing
spatial cuts, internal relative time, boundary responses and memory,
with exact conditions for mutual recovery.
Here a cut row expresses a literal exterior and hidden relational
partition; update counts give internal relative time; boundary comparison
gives overlap clock factors; retained acquired relations determine
the later decoder interface. Theorems 3.3–8.3 connect these expressions
only under their declared acquisition, continuity, progress and retention
premises.

Remaining mathematical targets include cut-specific acquisition with
sharp memory and bit-work bounds; characterization of all sound finite
acquisition transcripts rather than two sufficient certificate languages;
progress under bounded-rate or prescribed-window interference;
randomized guarantees with declared error and scheduler quantifiers;
entry recovery for general covering cuts with no pre-first-Read updates;
and exact recoverability under richer retained-certificate interfaces.
A bound per Read gap is not a total interference budget. The deterministic
all-fair-history obstruction does not settle those different domains.

Authentic handoffs, designated origins, birth-record acquisition and
physical service realization remain additional obligations.
Neither the full-tree upper construction nor the overlap criterion
establishes a smallest relational structure or minimal cut-memory
acquisition. No identification with physical space or duration, or
completion of the broad mutual-recovery target, follows.

